0.7.1:
[x] - Add a remote sleep lock feature. The mothership POSTs /api/instance-lock
      (bearer = the box's mothership token, not admin — an admin could unlock
      themselves); the instance stores it in a SiteSetting so it survives a
      restart, blocks turns before the paywall gate, and shows a non-dismissible
      upgrade modal. Browser learns three ways: server-injected on first paint,
      a 10s poll, and an instance_locked WS frame. Fails open — a down DB must
      never lock a paying user out. Notes: docs/dev/instance_sleep_lock.md

[x] - Add "Capped Tool Mode". A single grep_files match inside a minified vendor
      bundle returned one 294,807-char ToolMessage (~74k tokens). Anything bigger
      than the 30k keep-tail can never be compacted away — the thread
      re-summarized forever and the user watched a spinner for 18 minutes.
      grep_files now caps in BYTES (a line-count cap is what let this ship), and
      cap_tool_result bounds EVERY tool result at both agent shapes:
      ToolResultSizeLimitMiddleware in build_leonardo_agent (create_agent) and
      CappedToolNode for the raw StateGraph agents. Truncation is head-and-tail
      with a notice, so the agent can tell partial from broken. Third incident of
      this class — the previous two fixes each capped the one input we happened
      to be thinking about. Notes: app/agents/utils/tool_output_limits.py

[x] - Harden summarization against the same loop. A message too big for the keep
      tail is now shrunk instead of preserved forever, and a summary that comes
      back as tool-call markup (DeepSeek emitted <｜｜DSML｜｜tool_calls> as the
      stored summary) is rejected rather than saved as the thread's memory.

[x] - Add settings for enabling/disabling Leo tabs, VS Code default OFF. One
      setting owns the Code tab and the code-server container: /api/vscode/*
      (engineer-or-admin) starts/stops it via nsenter on the host, compose gets
      profiles: ["code"] so a reboot or bin/update never brings it back, and
      reconcile_vscode at startup stops it if it is running while the setting
      says off. Boot never STARTS the editor — an editor that isn't running
      can't be attacked. Tab visibility ships as a pure isTabVisible() helper;
      a hidden tab also skips its iframe src, or the browser retries a dead
      address in the background. Absent/malformed setting = show everything.

[x] - Add RunPod + Fireworks model entries. qwen3-8b-runpod (vLLM on our own
      GPU; base_url is env-only, never committed — the pod URL is rotatable) and
      nemotron-lightning-30b-fireworks (same weights on serverless, a sibling
      entry so who bills the turn stays the user's choice). Both use
      ChatDeepSeekWithReasoning — Fireworks returns thinking in a separate
      reasoning_content field and a bare ChatOpenAI drops it — and neither sets
      max_tokens, because reasoning bills against that cap while being stripped
      from the response, so a small cap returns empty content with no error.

[x] - Fix a substituted model running silently. get_llm swaps a policy-disabled
      model for the box default; the dropdown kept showing the user's pick and
      the only trace was a container WARNING. Live here 2026-08-16: every answer
      came from Muse while the UI said Nemotron, including "who made you?".
      Now a model_substituted frame raises a banner above the composer, asking
      the same question get_llm answers rather than re-deriving the rule.

[x] - Fix the recurring problems observed across all Leo apps (the 0.7.0
      friction reports):
      * Absolute paths resolved to the wrong file. The leading-slash guard
        re-rooted every absolute path under RAILS_ROOT a second time, so
        /app/app/rails/db/schema.rb became .../app/rails/db/schema.rb. Only
        relative paths under app/ survived — hence "read_file works for app/ but
        not spec/, db/ or config/", and agents falling back to `bash cat`
        (unbounded output, feeding the loop above). Largest cluster: 8 reports,
        4 boxes.
      * Two edit_file calls in one message silently lost one. Tool calls in a
        message run concurrently, both read the same base content, last write
        wins — and each reports success against what it read.
      * write_file succeeded on a file the running Rails app could not see. The
        Rails container bind-mounts only part of the project, so a new
        config/initializers/*.rb never loaded and the agent debugged code that
        was never running. A new file is now checked against the container
        rather than against a hardcoded mount list.
      * A payload with no agent_name crashed inside the stream loop with
        'NoneType' has no attribute 'astream' (an unknown agent already raised a
        clear error; the missing key did not).
      * Orphaned-tool-call repair was wired into rails_agent only, so every
        other graph could still hard-400 with "insufficient tool messages"
        forever. Now on all of them, with a structural test that fails any new
        agent that forgets it.
      * A stale cached sub-module could kill the whole chat panel: chat.html
        cache-busts index.js but not the 36 modules it imports, so one missing
        method threw inside initComponents. safeInit() contains it to one
        feature instead of the panel.

[x] - Add per-turn timing telemetry. We shipped token usage but not one
      duration, so "Leo is slow" arrived with no way to separate a slow provider
      from a huge prompt from a slow box. TurnMetrics records the turn as
      segments and derives tokens/sec over DECODE time only (folding in
      time-to-first-token makes a fast model look slow on a long prompt) with
      overhead clamped at zero (concurrent tools can out-sum the wall clock).

[x] - Stop dev-box test output landing in production telemetry. 94 of the 140
      instance_errors rows tagged 0.7.0 were synthetic — summarization fixtures
      and the WS integration harness. An env kill switch, set for the whole
      suite in conftest.py.

[x] - Add the Messages tab unread badge. The count is posted up from the Rails
      iframe — the chat window and the Rails app are different origins, so the
      chat window cannot count them itself and the render path has to be
      defensive about whatever arrives on that channel.
